\title{The Erd\H{o}s Extremal Function for Property B: \\
A Counting Foundation and the Exact Values $m(1)=1$ and $m(2)=3$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A hypergraph has \emph{Property B} when its vertices can be two-coloured so that
no edge is monochromatic. The \emph{Erd\H{o}s extremal function} $m(k)$ is the
minimum number of edges of a $k$-uniform hypergraph that fails Property~B. We
develop the theory of $m(k)$ from first principles on an exact, finitary
foundation. Two Boolean-lattice counting identities---the number of subsets of an
$N$-element ground set containing (respectively, disjoint from) a fixed set $S$
is $2^{N-|S|}$---yield, via a union bound over colourings, a sharp existence
theorem: every hypergraph whose edges each have at least $k \ge 1$ vertices and
which has fewer than $2^{k-1}$ edges admits a proper two-colouring. As an
immediate consequence we obtain the classical \emph{Erd\H{o}s lower bound}
$m(k) \ge 2^{k-1}$. We then package $m(k)$ as a genuine extremal number (an
infimum over all vertex counts) and determine its first two exact values. For
$k=1$ the single vertex-edge gives $m(1)=1$. For $k=2$ we prove the sharp
lower-bound step---\emph{every graph with at most two edges is
two-colourable}---which upgrades the generic bound $m(2)\ge 2$ to $m(2)\ge 3$;
combined with the triangle as an explicit minimum-edge witness this yields
$m(2)=3$. All results are established constructively, with the extremal witnesses
exhibited explicitly and their non-two-colourability verified by exhaustive
finite check.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Let $H$ be a hypergraph: a finite ground set $V$ of \emph{vertices} together with
a finite family of \emph{edges}, each edge being a subset of $V$. A
\emph{two-colouring} is a map $c : V \to \{\text{red}, \text{blue}\}$,
conveniently encoded by its red set $R = c^{-1}(\text{red}) \subseteq V$. The
colouring is \emph{proper} for $H$ if no edge is monochromatic, i.e. for every
edge $e$,
\[
e \not\subseteq R \qquad\text{and}\qquad e \cap R \ne \varnothing.
\]
A hypergraph admitting a proper two-colouring is said to \emph{have Property~B}
(Bernstein, 1908). A hypergraph is \emph{$k$-uniform} if every edge has exactly
$k$ vertices.

In 1963 Erd\H{o}s asked for the minimum number of edges needed to defeat every
two-colouring, defining the extremal function
\[
m(k) = \min\{\,|H| : H \text{ is $k$-uniform and has no proper two-colouring}\,\}.
\]
He proved, by the probabilistic method, that $m(k) \ge 2^{k-1}$. Determining
$m(k)$ exactly is a notoriously hard problem: the known exact values are
$m(1)=1$, $m(2)=3$, and $m(3)=7$ (the Fano plane), while for $k \ge 4$ only
bounds are known. The best general lower bound, due to Radhakrishnan and
Srinivasan (2000), is $m(k) = \Omega\!\big(2^{k}\sqrt{k/\log k}\big)$; the best
upper bound is $O(k^{2} 2^{k})$.

This paper gives a complete, self-contained development of the foundations of
$m(k)$ and determines its first two exact values. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item A finitary counting foundation (Section~\ref{sec:counting}): the two
    Boolean-lattice interval identities and the sharp existence theorem via a
    deterministic union bound over the $2^{N}$ colourings.
  \item The Erd\H{o}s lower bound, repackaged (Section~\ref{sec:lower}):
    $2^{k-1} \le m(k)$.
  \item The extremal function as an infimum (Section~\ref{sec:extremal}).
  \item Exact values (Sections~\ref{sec:mone}--\ref{sec:mtwo}): $m(1)=1$ and
    $m(2)=3$, the heart being the sharp $k=2$ lower-bound step.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Notation and conventions}

Throughout, $V$ is finite; we take $V = \{0, 1, \dots, N-1\}$ without loss of
generality. Edges are subsets of $V$, and a hypergraph $H$ is a finite family of
such subsets. We write $|X|$ for cardinality, $X \setminus Y$ for set difference,
$X^{c} = V \setminus X$ for complement within $V$, and $\PV(V)$ for the power set.
Two sets are \emph{disjoint}, written $X \cap Y = \varnothing$, when they share no
element. All quantities are natural numbers.

\section{The counting foundation}\label{sec:counting}

The entire theory rests on two elementary but sharp cardinality identities for
intervals in the Boolean lattice $\PV(V)$.

\subsection{Interval counts}

\begin{lemma}[Superset count]\label{lem:superset}
Let $V$ be a ground set and $S \subseteq V$. The number of subsets $A \subseteq V$
with $S \subseteq A$ is exactly $2^{|V| - |S|}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Restriction to the complement gives a bijection
\[
\{A \subseteq V : S \subseteq A\} \;\longleftrightarrow\; \PV(V \setminus S),
\qquad A \mapsto A \setminus S,
\]
with inverse $B \mapsto B \cup S$. The map is well defined because
$A \setminus S \subseteq V \setminus S$; injective because $S \subseteq A, B$
forces $A = (A \setminus S) \cup S$; and surjective because for
$B \subseteq V \setminus S$ the set $A = B \cup S$ satisfies $S \subseteq A$ and
$A \setminus S = B$. Hence the count is
$|\PV(V \setminus S)| = 2^{|V \setminus S|} = 2^{|V| - |S|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Disjointness count]\label{lem:disjoint}
Let $V$ be a ground set and $S \subseteq V$. The number of subsets $A \subseteq V$
with $S \cap A = \varnothing$ is exactly $2^{|V| - |S|}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Complementation $A \mapsto V \setminus A$ is an involution of $\PV(V)$ that sends
$\{A : S \cap A = \varnothing\}$ bijectively onto $\{A : S \subseteq A\}$, since
$S \cap A = \varnothing \iff S \subseteq V \setminus A$. Apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:superset}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The union bound and the existence theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp existence of a proper two-colouring]\label{thm:existence}
Let $k \ge 1$ and let $H$ be a hypergraph on $V = \{0,\dots,N-1\}$ in which every
edge has at least $k$ vertices. If $|H| < 2^{\,k-1}$, then $H$ has a proper
two-colouring: there is a red set $R \subseteq V$ such that every edge $e \in H$
satisfies $e \not\subseteq R$ and $e \cap R \ne \varnothing$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix an edge $e$ with $|e| \ge k$. A colouring $R$ is \emph{bad for $e$} if
$e \subseteq R$ (all red) or $e \cap R = \varnothing$ (all blue). By
Lemma~\ref{lem:superset} the number of $R \subseteq V$ with $e \subseteq R$ is
$2^{N - |e|} \le 2^{N-k}$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:disjoint} the number with
$e \cap R = \varnothing$ is likewise $\le 2^{N-k}$. Hence the number of colourings
bad for $e$ is at most $2^{N-k} + 2^{N-k} = 2^{\,N-k+1}$. Summing (union bound)
over the fewer than $2^{k-1}$ edges, the number of colourings bad for
\emph{some} edge is at most
\[
|H| \cdot 2^{\,N-k+1} \;<\; 2^{\,k-1}\cdot 2^{\,N-k+1} \;=\; 2^{N}.
\]
Since there are exactly $2^{N}$ colourings in total, at least one colouring is
bad for no edge; that colouring is proper.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Single edge]\label{cor:single}
Any single edge of size $\ge 2$ is two-colourable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Take $H = \{e\}$ with $|e| \ge 2 = k$, so $|H| = 1 < 2^{2-1} = 2$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:existence}.
\end{proof}

\section{Non-two-colourability and the Erd\H{o}s lower bound}\label{sec:lower}

\begin{definition}\label{def:nontwo}
A hypergraph $H$ on $V$ is \emph{non-two-colourable} if it has no proper
two-colouring; equivalently, for every red set $R \subseteq V$ there is an edge
$e \in H$ with $e \subseteq R$ or $e \cap R = \varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Edge lower bound]\label{thm:edgelower}
Let $k \ge 1$ and let $H$ be a hypergraph each of whose edges has at least $k$
vertices. If $H$ is non-two-colourable, then $|H| \ge 2^{\,k-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $|H| < 2^{k-1}$, Theorem~\ref{thm:existence} produces a proper two-colouring
$R$, contradicting non-two-colourability, which supplies a monochromatic edge
under that very $R$.
\end{proof}

\section{The extremal function as an infimum}\label{sec:extremal}

\begin{definition}\label{def:mset}
For $k \in \mathbb{N}$ let
\[
\MM(k) = \Big\{\, c \in \mathbb{N} : \exists\, N,\ \exists\ H \text{ on }
\{0,\dots,N-1\},\ (\forall e \in H,\ |e| = k),\ |H| = c,\
H \text{ non-two-colourable} \,\Big\},
\]
and define the \emph{Erd\H{o}s extremal function} $m(k) = \inf \MM(k)$.
\end{definition}

Because $\MM(k) \subseteq \mathbb{N}$, the infimum is attained whenever $\MM(k)$
is nonempty, and $m(k) = \min \MM(k)$.

\begin{theorem}[Lower bound for the extremal function]\label{thm:msetge}
For $k \ge 1$, every $c \in \MM(k)$ satisfies $c \ge 2^{k-1}$. Consequently, if
$\MM(k) \ne \varnothing$ then $m(k) \ge 2^{\,k-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $c \in \MM(k)$ is witnessed by a $k$-uniform non-two-colourable $H$ with
$|H| = c$, then every edge has exactly $k \ge k$ vertices, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:edgelower} gives $c = |H| \ge 2^{k-1}$. A lower bound for every
element of a nonempty set of naturals bounds its infimum.
\end{proof}

\section{The value $m(1) = 1$}\label{sec:mone}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mone}
$m(1) = 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Upper bound.} Take $V = \{0\}$ and $H = \{\{0\}\}$. For any $R \subseteq
\{0\}$ either $R = \varnothing$ (the edge $\{0\}$ is all blue) or $R = \{0\}$
(the edge is all red); either way the edge is monochromatic, so $H$ is
non-two-colourable and $1 \in \MM(1)$, giving $m(1) \le 1$.

\emph{Lower bound.} Since $\MM(1)\ne\varnothing$, Theorem~\ref{thm:msetge} with
$k=1$ gives $m(1) \ge 2^{0} = 1$. Hence $m(1) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The value $m(2) = 3$}\label{sec:mtwo}

For graphs the generic bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:msetge} only gives
$m(2) \ge 2$. The exact value requires a sharper lower-bound step together with a
matching witness.

\subsection{The sharp lower-bound step}

\begin{theorem}[Every graph with $\le 2$ edges is two-colourable]\label{thm:twoedge}
Let $H$ be a $2$-uniform hypergraph on $V$ with $|H| \le 2$. Then $H$ has a
proper two-colouring.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We treat the possible edge-counts.
\begin{itemize}
  \item $|H| = 0$: any $R$ is vacuously proper.
  \item $|H| = 1$: the single $2$-vertex edge is two-colourable by
    Corollary~\ref{cor:single}.
  \item $|H| = 2$: write $H = \{e_1, e_2\}$ with $|e_1| = |e_2| = 2$.
  \begin{itemize}
    \item If $e_1 \cap e_2 \ne \varnothing$, pick $v \in e_1 \cap e_2$ and set
      $R = \{v\}$. Each $e_i$ contains $v \in R$, and since $|e_i| = 2 > 1$ each
      $e_i$ has a vertex outside $R$; so $R$ is proper.
    \item If $e_1 \cap e_2 = \varnothing$, write $e_1 = \{a_1, b_1\}$,
      $e_2 = \{a_2, b_2\}$ (four distinct vertices) and set $R = \{a_1, a_2\}$.
      Then $a_i \in e_i \cap R$ and $b_i \in e_i \setminus R$; so $R$ is proper.
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
In every case a proper two-colouring exists.
\end{proof}

Structurally, two distinct $2$-element edges cannot close a cycle (a cycle needs
$\ge 3$ edges), so such a graph is a forest, hence bipartite; the case analysis
makes the bipartition explicit.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nottwo}
A $2$-uniform hypergraph with $\le 2$ edges is not non-two-colourable.
Consequently every $c \in \MM(2)$ satisfies $c \ge 3$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is Theorem~\ref{thm:twoedge} with Definition~\ref{def:nontwo}. If
$c \in \MM(2)$ were $\le 2$, its witnessing graph would contradict this.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The triangle witness}

\begin{definition}\label{def:triangle}
The \emph{triangle} is the graph on $V = \{0,1,2\}$ with edge set
$T = \{\{0,1\}, \{1,2\}, \{0,2\}\}$; it is $2$-uniform with $3$ edges.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:triangle}
The triangle $T$ is non-two-colourable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For any $R \subseteq \{0,1,2\}$ either $|R| \ge 2$, so two vertices are red and
the edge between them is $\subseteq R$; or $|R| \le 1$, so two vertices are blue
and the edge between them is disjoint from $R$. In either case some edge is
monochromatic, so no $R$ is proper. (Exhaustively over the $2^3 = 8$ colourings.)
\end{proof}

\subsection{The exact value}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mtwo}
$m(2) = 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:triangle}, $3 \in \MM(2)$, so $m(2) \le 3$. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:nottwo}, every element of $\MM(2)$ is $\ge 3$, so
$m(2) \ge 3$. Hence $m(2) = 3$, realised uniquely up to isomorphism by the
triangle.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Exhaustive census.} Given a hypergraph on $N$ vertices, iterate over all
$2^{N}$ red sets and return the first proper one, or report
non-two-colourability. This decides two-colourability in $O(2^{N}\|H\|)$ time and
underlies Lemma~\ref{lem:triangle}.

\textbf{Union-bound check.} Given $k$ and $|H|$, test $|H| < 2^{k-1}$; if it
holds, Theorem~\ref{thm:existence} certifies colourability in $O(1)$ arithmetic.

\textbf{Extremal search.} Enumerate $k$-uniform hypergraphs in increasing
edge-count order, testing each by the census, to locate the sparsest
non-two-colourable system and hence $m(k)$ for small $k$.

\section{Applications and context}

Theorem~\ref{thm:existence} is the prototypical positive-probability existence
argument: bound a single-event failure, sum over the events, conclude the total
is $<1$. The same skeleton underlies the Erd\H{o}s--Ramsey lower bound, graphs of
high girth and chromatic number, and coding theory. A proper two-colouring is
exactly a satisfying assignment of a monotone \emph{not-all-equal SAT} instance,
so $m(k)$ measures the sparsest unsatisfiable such instance. Finally, the easy
non-constructive bound $m(k)\ge 2^{k-1}$ contrasts sharply with the difficulty of
explicit constructions: $m(k)$ is open for all $k \ge 4$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

We laid an exact, finitary foundation for Property~B and used it to package the
extremal function $m(k)$ as a genuine extremal number, determining $m(1)=1$ and
$m(2)=3$; the decisive new step is the sharp $k=2$ lower bound
(Theorem~\ref{thm:twoedge}). Natural continuations:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{$m(3)=7$ via the Fano plane:} realise it on $7$ vertices, verify
    non-two-colourability, and prove the matching lower bound by ruling out every
    $3$-uniform system with $\le 6$ edges.
  \item \textbf{Monotonicity and unconditional nonemptiness:} show $m$ is weakly
    increasing and that the complete $k$-uniform hypergraph on $2k-1$ vertices is
    non-two-colourable, so $\MM(k)\ne\varnothing$ for all $k \ge 1$.
  \item \textbf{Radhakrishnan--Srinivasan:} upgrade the bound to
    $m(k)=\Omega\!\big(2^{k}\sqrt{k/\log k}\big)$ via a semi-random colouring.
  \item \textbf{$r$-colourings:} swap the powerset census for an $r$-ary function
    count to obtain $m_r(k) \gtrsim r^{k-1}$.
\end{enumerate}

